{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778782711,"nonce":"kDmHVLDqQTfhoVhSXixmS3XJ8Kg=","message":"This should be treated as important because the local pre-push fallback keeps git-bug refs synchronized but adds user-visible latency to ordinary pushes. The local hook can cache a `refs/bugs/*` fingerprint to avoid redundant pushes, but daemon-side sync is still the right end state.\n\nRecommended design:\n\n1. Track registered repositories in Mandible with a `git_bug_sync` policy: `disabled`, `push_only`, or eventually `bidirectional`.\n2. Maintain a per-repo local fingerprint of `refs/bugs/*` using `git for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname)' refs/bugs`.\n3. On a timer, compare the current fingerprint with the last successful sync fingerprint. If unchanged, skip the repo without invoking network Git.\n4. If changed, push `refs/bugs/*:refs/bugs/*` using Mandible's non-interactive/agent authentication path, then update the fingerprint only after success.\n5. Surface sync status in the UI: last success time, last error, dirty/changed refs, and a manual \"sync now\" action.\n6. Avoid holding git-bug's own lock while running network pushes; operate at the Git ref level where possible.\n7. Keep local hooks as a fallback, but prefer daemon sync for repos registered with Mandible.\n\nPhase 2 can add fetch/bidirectional merge once multi-user CRDT sync semantics are well tested. For now, push-only change-conditional sync would remove most YubiKey and latency friction from everyday agent/user issue work.\n\nCross-ref: dotfiles issue `4d34f0c` adds a local fingerprint guard to the pre-push fallback.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}